You can find code for how to constrain standardized effects in an RI-CLPM here (under Extension 1, you will see three tabs, one is for standardized constrained effects):

https://jeroendmulder.github.io/RI-CLPM/lavaan.html#The_RI-CLPM

On Friday, March 29, 2024 at 8:05:57 AM UTC-7 kien...@gmail.com wrote:
> Hello everyone,
>
> I have a RI-CLPM (RICLPM5 from Moulder & Hamaker <https://jeroendmulder.github.io/RI-CLPM/lavaan.html>) with four measurement occasions and constraints over time. I have to compute 2*9 models where x-parameters always have the same scale, but the scales of the y-parameters are different from x-parameters and differ from the first 9 models to the second 9 models.
> Hence, I am interested in having not only constrained cross-lagged Estimates, but them also standardized.
>
> The "Estimates" are nicely equivalent, but the Std.all-values differ having "standardized=T" in the summary-function, e.g.:
>
> Regressions: Estimate Std.Err z-value P(>|z|) Std.lv Std.all wx2 ~ wx1 (a) 0.621 0.064 9.716 0.000 0.485 0.485 wy1 (b) -0.112 0.103 -1.082 0.279 -0.068 -0.068 wy2 ~ wx1 (c) 0.051 0.047 1.086 0.277 0.070 0.070 wy1 (d) 0.184 0.069 2.671 0.008 0.199 0.199 wx3 ~ wx2 (a) 0.621 0.064 9.716 0.000 0.577 0.577 wy2 (b) -0.112 0.103 -1.082 0.279 -0.059 -0.059 wy3 ~ wx2 (c) 0.051 0.047 1.086 0.277 0.090 0.090 wy2 (d) 0.184 0.069 2.671 0.008 0.185 0.185 wx4 ~ wx3 (a) 0.621 0.064 9.716 0.000 0.606 0.606 wy3 (b) -0.112 0.103 -1.082 0.279 -0.057 -0.057 wy4 ~ wx3 (c) 0.051 0.047 1.086 0.277 0.097 0.097 wy3 (d) 0.184 0.069 2.671 0.008 0.184 0.184
>
> I thought about standardizing all parameters before they enter the model (e.g., with "std.ov=T") and reporting the (constrained) "Estimates" and still call them "Beta" (for the standardized regression coefficient), but I don't know if this is valid / truly equivalent.
>
> Can you help me finding the true standardized and constrained estimates?
>
> PS:
> The Model Syntax (from Moulder&Hamaker):
> RICLPM5 <- '
>   # Create between components (random intercepts)
>   RIx =~ 1*x1 + 1*x2 + 1*x3 + 1*x4
>   RIy =~ 1*y1 + 1*y2 + 1*y3 + 1*y4
>     # Create within-person centered variables
>   wx1 =~ 1*x1
>   wx2 =~ 1*x2
>   wx3 =~ 1*x3
>   wx4 =~ 1*x4
>   wy1 =~ 1*y1
>   wy2 =~ 1*y2
>   wy3 =~ 1*y3
>   wy4 =~ 1*y4
>
>   # Estimate lagged effects between within-person centered variables
>   # (constrained)
>   wx2 ~ a*wx1 + b*wy1
>   wy2 ~ c*wx1 + d*wy1
>   wx3 ~ a*wx2 + b*wy2
>   wy3 ~ c*wx2 + d*wy2
>   wx4 ~ a*wx3 + b*wy3
>   wy4 ~ c*wx3 + d*wy3
>
>   # Estimate covariances between residuals of within-person centered variables
>   # (i.e., innovations, constrained)
>   wx2 ~~ cov*wy2
>   wx3 ~~ cov*wy3
>   wx4 ~~ cov*wy4
>
>   # Estimate covariance between within-person centered variables at first wave
>   wx1 ~~ wy1 # Covariance
>
>   # Estimate variance and covariance of random intercepts
>   RIx ~~ RIx
>   RIy ~~ RIy
>   RIx ~~ RIy
>
>   # Estimate (residual) variance of within-person centered variables
>   # (constrained)
>   wx1 ~~ wx1 # Variance
>   wy1 ~~ wy1
>   wx2 ~~ vx*wx2 # Residual variance
>   wy2 ~~ vy*wy2
>   wx3 ~~ vx*wx3
>   wy3 ~~ vy*wy3
>   wx4 ~~ vx*wx4
>   wy4 ~~ vy*wy4
>
>   # Constrain grand means over time
>   x1 + x2 + x3 + x4  ~ mx*1
>   y1 + y2 + y3 + y4  ~ my*1'

